{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent upward bias in realized prices vs model from ~day 10 onward: model underestimates sustained rallies (true path trends higher and stays elevated while model stays near 68–70). Apply a positive drift correction that increases with horizon (small at 1–5d, larger at 10–30d).",
    "Trend/lag error: model reacts too slowly to regime shifts (fails to follow the step-up from ~69 to 72–73). Add a trend-following component: if last 5–10d slope is positive, lift the whole forward curve, especially mid/long horizons.",
    "Spike underestimation: on strong up-move days the model predicts only modest bumps (e.g., around the 72–73 area). Use a convexity adjustment: when recent volatility or 1–3d returns are high and positive, increase forecast amplitude (don’t cap near recent mean).",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration at long horizon: model often assumes reversion back toward ~69–70 while outcomes remain elevated (or only revert late). Reduce mean-reversion strength beyond ~10d during uptrends; delay reversion timing.",
    "Horizon-specific: 1–5d forecasts are relatively close (small errors), but 10–25d errors are systematically negative (under-forecast). Apply piecewise bias: near-term ~0, mid-term +1 to +2 EUR, long-term +2 to +4 EUR (scaled by recent trend strength).",
    "Occasional late-horizon overshoot when model finally turns bullish (e.g., projecting 72–74) but realized prices revert (Example 5 tail). Add a terminal damping rule: if forecast implies acceleration while recent realized momentum is flattening, cap the last 5–10d forecasts toward the prevailing level."
  ]
}
